{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1726 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "2cbab6036356cf661d1bbc0697ea87f01a840ba75e0f0cdbcbce850ffd712933" :edb/db-before-t 1725 :edb/db-after-t 1726 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535038 50 #inst "2026-10-08T15:28:46.799Z" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133390 1100 "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B)$, where\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535038 false] [17592186133390 1100 "Find $5\\det(A)-\\det(B),$ where \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/65df227e4fff9e934cec11be0a0262a1093a82270f3c9b0b7970fa2c700151aa.png)" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133390 1145 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/23865048a3c2ad60dd3b51fe78c44ee368692172300814c3fb061f447266e233.png)\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal: \n\n$$\n\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133390 1145 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & \\sqrt{13} & 4 \\\\ 4 & 11 & 4 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 9 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{bmatrix}$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, its determinant is given by the product of the elements of the main diagonal:\n$\\det(B) = 3 \\cdot (-4) \\cdot (-2) = 24$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n5\\det(A)-\\det(B) = 5 \\cdot 0 - 24 = -24\n$$" 13194139535038 false] [17592186133391 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139535038 false] [17592186133391 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/d95e60cbfa50178878e719166ace3dbff90c3cc5531acfc046d8354320cd4b00.png)" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133391 1145 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & \\sqrt{3} & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$.\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so\n$\\mid B \\mid = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0$.\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\sqrt{2} & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\nAs a result, $\\mid C \\mid = 0$.\n\nTherefore, only $\\mid A \\mid$ and $\\mid C \\mid$ are equal to $0$." 13194139535038 false] [17592186133391 1145 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/3f434912c97c20d04f703253fe41c169497cae82657cfbd0d8b548985549fe64.png)\nAs a result, $|A| = 0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, so \n\n$$\n|B| = (-1) \\cdot 4 \\cdot (-3) = 12 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has a zero row: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/5f41ff4f7fe5aeae5865bef9ab16d5d97a3513277f70b797c4167882093ed079.png)\nAs a result, $|C| = 0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133392 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/ccc50eec6218625f22e8372d6f0b1dbf0da0aa921dff237e4e1b8ea3761bdfc7.png)\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0.$\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows\n\n- $A$ has identical columns" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133392 1100 "$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $\\mid A|=0$.\n\n1. $A$ has a zero column\n2. $A$ has proportional rows\n3. $A$ has identical columns" 13194139535038 false] [17592186133392 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $A$ has proportional rows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 8 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535038 false] [17592186133392 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/2a155f75e1e9c099540eeac555cb20fde5037837d9e5fc9f976bbca15369b51e.png)\n\n- $A$ has proportional rows: \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14047809/assets/1447045fa5b7875defffdf52d2ae8cf876ee0c5da6ac07cc37bc1c5525fec9c6.png)\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535038 true] [17592186133834 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328208 13194139535038 true] [17592186133834 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328215 13194139535038 true] [17592186133834 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328250 13194139535038 true] [17592186133834 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328264 13194139535038 true] [17592186133835 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328222 13194139535038 true] [17592186133835 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328243 13194139535038 true] [17592186133835 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328257 13194139535038 true] [17592186133836 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328229 13194139535038 true] [17592186133836 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328236 13194139535038 true] [17592186133836 1020 #edb/ref 17592186328271 13194139535038 true] [17592186134318 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535038 true] [17592186134318 1100 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $|A|=0?$\n\n- $A$ has a zero column\n- $A$ has identical columns\n- $A$ has identical rows" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134318 1100 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrix given above. Identify **all** of the reasons why one can conclude that $\\mid A|= 0$?\n\n1. $A$ has a zero column\n2. $A$ has identical columns\n3. $A$ has identical rows" 13194139535038 false] [17592186134318 1145 "Let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\,✓\n$$\n\n- $A$ does *not* have identical columns.\n\n- $A$ has identical rows: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134319 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535038 true] [17592186134319 1100 "Find $4det(A) + 4det(B)$, where\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}, B = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$." 13194139535038 false] [17592186134319 1100 "Find $4det(A)+4det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134319 1145 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- For the matrix $A,$ we have\n\n$$\ndet(A)=2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}-1\\cdot 1=3.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has proportional columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $4det(A)+4det(B)=4\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 0=12.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134332 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535038 true] [17592186134332 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & 2 & -1\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{3}; \\sqrt[5]{2}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}, & B|= & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}4; 0\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}4\\begin{vmatrix}, & C|= & 1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{vmatrix}4; 2\\begin{vmatrix}3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}4 \\mid$" 13194139535038 false] [17592186134332 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}|\n$$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134332 1145 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero row: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & \\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\sqrt[5]{2} & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- The matrix $B$ is a triangular matrix, hence \n\n$$\n|B|=1\\cdot 3\\cdot 4=12\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has proportional columns: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\sqrt{2} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 8 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134333 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535038 true] [17592186134333 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0$?\n\n$\\begin{vmatrix}A|= & 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}1; \\sqrt{5}\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0\\begin{vmatrix}, & B|= & 3 & 0\\end{vmatrix}0; 0\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{vmatrix}5\\begin{vmatrix}, & C|= & 4 & \\sqrt{3}\\end{vmatrix}4; 2\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{vmatrix}1 \\mid$" 13194139535038 false] [17592186134333 1100 "Which of the following determinants are equal to $0?$\n\n$$\n|A|=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|,\\,|B|=|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}|,\\,|C|=|\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}|\n$$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134333 1145 "Let's examine each of the given determinants in turn.\n\n- The matrix $A$ has a zero column: \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\sqrt{5} & 0 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|A|=0.$\n\n- Since $B$ is a diagonal matrix, we obtain \n\n$$\n|B|=3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5=-15\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has identical columns: \n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}4 & \\sqrt{3} & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & \\sqrt{5} & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nAs a result, $|C|=0.$\n\nTherefore, only $|A|$ and $|C|$ are equal to $0.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134339 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535038 true] [17592186134339 1100 "Let $A$ be a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix such that $\\mid A|= 0$. Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $A$ has two identical rows\n2. One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n3. $A$ has two identical columns" 13194139535038 false] [17592186134339 1100 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $|A|=0.$ Which of the following statements must be true?\n\n- $A$ has two identical rows\n- One of the rows/columns of $A$ is proportional to another row/column of $A$\n- $A$ has two identical columns" 13194139535038 true] [17592186134339 1145 "Let's consider our statements in turn.\n\n- Statements I and III are false. For example, consider the matrix \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe have that \n\n$$\n|A|=1\\cdot 8-4\\cdot 2=0\n$$\n\nbut $A$ has neither two identical rows nor two identical columns.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we can make use of the following fact:\n\n$|A|=0$ *if and only if one of the rows/columns of $A$ is a linear combination of other rows/columns of $A.$*\n\nIn the case of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, this means that one of the rows/columns must be proportional to another row/column.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1017 #edb/ref 17592186328209 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1089 "q-140044" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1100 "Find $det(A)-3det(B)$, where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1113 #uuid "8488a5ef-d0fb-5e97-a97c-9754e4ebd25d" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328208 1145 "Let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- Since $A$ has two identical columns, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(A)=0.\n$$\n\n- Since $B$ has a zero row, we obtain:\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\,⟹\\,det(B)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $det(A)-3det(B)=0-3\\cdot 0=0.$" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328210 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328211 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328212 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328213 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1000 #edb/ref 17592186328214 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1041 "selection" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1104 #edb/ref 17592186328212 13194139535038 true] [17592186328209 1110 #uuid "d256f172-51d9-594b-bf4a-b31d806d4841" 13194139535038 true] [17592186328210 1034 "-72" 13194139535038 t